{"type":"text","timestamp":1788463081469,"sessionID":"ses_f974b455effeE0eY9NZtMZGgGP","part":{"id":"prt_068b4e27f001XeuVKGeEc1EC9w","messageID":"msg_068b4e11b001bWzet2Sup1TvYU","sessionID":"ses_f974b455effeE0eY9NZtMZGgGP","type":"text","text":"The test passes, which confirms that the fix is working correctly. The issue was that the original implementation used `strings.ReplaceAll` which removes all occurrences of the prefix, but the requirement is to only remove exactly one leading prefix if it exists.\n\nMy fix correctly implements the TrimPrefix function by:\n1. Checking if the string starts with the prefix using `strings.HasPrefix`\n2. If it does, removing only the first occurrence by slicing from the length of the prefix to the end\n3. If it doesn't, returning the original string unchanged\n\nThis matches the expected behavior from the test cases:\n- `TrimPrefix(\"abab\", \"ab\")` returns `\"ab\"` (removes only the leading \"ab\")\n- `TrimPrefix(\"zab\", \"ab\")` returns `\"zab\"` (no change since \"zab\" doesn't start with \"ab\")\n- `TrimPrefix(\"abc\", \"\")` returns `\"abc\"` (no change since empty prefix)","time":{"start":1788463080063,"end":1788463081467}}}
